 AD","module_padj":0.572565,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000087086","module":"turquoise","kme":0.577813,"hub":true,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.446504,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000087086","module":"turquoise","kme":0.357777,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.704234,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000087086","module":"turquoise","kme":0.471262,"hub":true,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.415372,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000087086","entrez_id":["2512"],"uniprot":["P02792"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes the light subunit of the ferritin protein. Ferritin is the major intracellular iron storage protein in prokaryotes and eukaryotes. It is composed of 24 subunits of the heavy and light ferritin chains. Variation in ferritin subunit composition may affect the rates of iron uptake and release in different tissues. A major function of ferritin is the storage of iron in a soluble and nontoxic state. Defects in this light chain ferritin gene are associated with several neurodegenerative diseases and hyperferritinemia-cataract syndrome. This gene has multiple pseudogenes. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P02792","text":"Stores iron in a soluble, non-toxic, readily available form. Important for iron homeostasis. Iron is taken up in the ferrous form and deposited as ferric hydroxides after oxidation. Also plays a role in delivery of iron to cells. Mediates iron uptake in capsule cells of the developing kidney (By similarity). Delivery to lysosomes by the cargo receptor NCOA4 for autophagic degradation and release or iron (PubMed:24695223)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}